  <p>Why the spread? Anytime Mailbox doesn't run the mailrooms; each location is an independent operator that sets its own plans and fees. More on that in <a href="/anytime-mailbox-pricing/">Anytime Mailbox pricing</a>.</p>

  <h2>A cheap address isn't a registered office</h2>
  <p>People often want a Delaware, Wyoming or Texas address because they're forming an LLC there. A virtual mailbox can be the company's <em>mailing</em> address, but each state sets its own rules for the registered office, and they ask for more than a mail drop:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Texas:</strong> the registered office "must be a physical address in Texas where the registered agent can be personally served with process during business hours. It cannot solely be the address of a mailbox service" <Src href={S.txFaq} label="Texas SOS" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Wyoming:</strong> the registered office must be "a street address in Wyoming which shall be a physical location" where the registered agent, or a person with an agency relationship with the agent, "is physically present" to accept service <Src href={S.wyTitle17} label="W.S. 17-28-101" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Delaware:</strong> every LLC must maintain a registered office in Delaware and a registered agent "having a business office identical with such registered office" <Src href={S.deLlc104} label="6 Del. C. §18-104" />.</li>
    <li><strong>Florida:</strong> the registered agent's business address must be "identical to the address of the registered office" <Src href={S.fl0113} label="§605.0113" />, and the articles must give the agent's "street address in this state" <Src href={S.fl0201} label="§605.0201" />.</li>
    <li><strong>California:</strong> the agent must be a California resident or a registered corporate agent, and the agent's "physical street address" is public record <Src href={S.caFaq} label="CA SOS" />.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>So the cheapest setup in these states is usually a registered agent plus a separate mailbox, or a mailbox plan that includes an agent. Virtual Post Mail has business addresses in Keller (TX), Brandon (FL), Claymont (DE) and Covina (CA), and includes a free registered agent in California, Delaware, Florida, Nevada and Texas on plans from $20 a month <Src href={S.vpmPricing} />. Details in <a href="/virtual-post-mail-pricing/">Virtual Post Mail pricing</a> and <a href="/virtual-mailbox-for-llc/">virtual mailbox for an LLC</a>.</p>

  <h2>Before you pick the $5.99 address</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Check the plan, not just the price.</strong> A low starting price often comes with a small mail allowance and per-scan fees. Compare what you'll actually open each month with <a href="/cheapest-virtual-mailbox/">the cheapest virtual mailbox</a> guide.</li>
    <li><strong>Add the Form 1583 cost.</strong> Every provider needs USPS Form 1583 before it can handle your mail; some charge for the notary. See <a href="/usps-form-1583-online-notarization/">Form 1583 and online notarization</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Some providers publish one price list.</strong> Traveling Mailbox's pricing page shows a single $15 Basic plan with 35 page scans included <Src href={S.tmb} />, and most of its customers use its home base in Sanford, NC <Src href={S.tmb} /> (<a href="/traveling-mailbox-pricing/">Traveling Mailbox pricing</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>PhysicalAddress.com also has company-run addresses</strong> in several of these states, with starting prices of $9.98 in Dallas, Orlando and Wilmington, $10.98 in Laguna Beach and $19.98 on Wall Street, plus $7.98 in Boise and $8.98 in Las Vegas. It includes a registered agent with the account <Src href={S.paLocations} /> <Src href={S.paRa} />. Details: <a href="/physicaladdress-pricing/">PhysicalAddress.com pricing</a>.</li>
  </ul>

